    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `RequirementPreparer.prepare_linked_requirement`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `prepare_linked_requirement` function is responsible for preparing a requirement to be obtained from a specified link. It takes two parameters: `req`, which is an `InstallRequirement` object representing the requirement to be prepared, and `parallel_builds`, a boolean indicating whether parallel builds are enabled. The function first checks if the relevant file is already available in the download directory. If it is, it marks the file as downloaded. If not, it attempts to fetch only the metadata for the requirement. If metadata fetching is successful, it marks the requirement as needing more preparation and returns the metadata distribution. If none of these optimizations work, it fully prepares the requirement. The function relies on several helper functions and methods, including `_log_preparing_link`, `_get_linked_req_hashes`, `_check_download_dir`, `_fetch_metadata_only`, and `_prepare_linked_requirement`. It handles errors or edge cases by checking for the presence of the file in the download directory and by attempting to fetch only the metadata if the file is not available. The function has no side effects.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or cau